     ◆ Edit notes.md

       60  That pressure favors stable layouts, parseable output modes, and documentation of keybindings and exit codes, ideas that the original teletype already implied when it treated the
           session as a transcript.
       61  The best modern TUIs keep a dual contract: a visual full-screen mode for people, and a stream or JSON mode for tools.
       62
       63  The terminal itself has also left the computer room.
       64  Cloud shells, browser-based emulators, and the integrated terminals of VS Code, JetBrains, and other editors mean that a TUI now often runs inside a graphical pane that is only
           pretending to be a serial device.
       65  That nesting is historically continuous with xterm on X11: the pixels belong to a host window, but the application still speaks a terminal protocol.
       66  It has consequences for design, because clipboard, fonts, ligatures, and click-to-position are supplied by the outer GUI, while the inner program still thinks in cells and escape
           sequences.
       67  The TUI therefore survives not only as a fallback for machines without a desktop, but as a portable application layer that can be embedded wherever a terminal emulator can be drawn.
       68
       69  Looking across sixty years, the TUI is less a relic than a recurring solution to the same constraints.
       70  Whenever the interface must travel as text, run on modest hardware, occupy a remote session, or sit beside a compiler and a shell, a character grid is enough—and often better than
           pixels.
       71  The lineage from Teletype to VT100 to curses to Turbo Vision to ncurses to ratatui is a lineage of abstraction over a stable idea: a rectangular array of cells, a cursor, a keyboard,
           and a disciplined redraw.
